WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:03.872
(fast percussion music)

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.860
- There's a whole world beneath the city,

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.573
visited by over four
million people each day.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.540
This urine scented metropolis is

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.893
not only home to pizza stealing rats,

00:00:21.274 --> 00:00:23.580
but one of the most diverse music

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.503
scenes in the United States.

00:00:26.408 --> 00:00:28.022
(upbeat piano music)

00:00:28.022 --> 00:00:31.105
(collective singing)

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.600
Artists come from all over the
world to play on the stage.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.940
When you turn a corner or exit a train,

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.690
you never know what kinds of
sounds you'll be met with.

00:00:44.147 --> 00:00:45.480
(R&amp;B music)

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.193
Sometimes it's classical violin.

00:00:47.193 --> 00:00:49.760
(slow violin music)

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.504
Sometimes it's EDM violin.

00:00:51.504 --> 00:00:54.504
(fast violin music)

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:01.610
So we're here in New York City,

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.100
we're about to run around and interview

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.400
a bunch of street performers
and seasoned buskers.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.826
I'm excited because I've never

00:01:08.826 --> 00:01:10.760
performed on the street before.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.970
Even though I've been performing and

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.860
playing the piano for about 20 years now.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.760
So we'll see what happens,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.393
and maybe later on I will get
a chance at busking myself.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.400
- [DeeWho] Good day ladies and gentlemen.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.369
My name is DeeWho and I'm
here with a song for you.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:34.869
(blues music)

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.780
- [Nahre] Street performance,
often called busking,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.740
goes back to ancient times.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.957
As author Patricia J. Campbell puts it,

00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:44.807
"There have been street
performers at least

00:01:44.807 --> 00:01:47.947
"as long as there have been streets."

00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:52.947
♪ Oh I said all the wrong things ♪

00:01:54.997 --> 00:01:57.510
♪ Oh I stood my ground ♪

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.650
- Just day to day, going
through being on the train,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.490
walking through the city, my
wish is always to be anonymous.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.880
But this is sort of the opposite of that.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.339
You're putting yourself out there,

00:02:08.339 --> 00:02:10.185
and I think it's really brave.

00:02:10.185 --> 00:02:13.425
♪ You were ignoring all the signs ♪

00:02:13.425 --> 00:02:17.640
♪ You took my hand and you set me down ♪

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.240
- [Nahre] There's a little bit of tension

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.780
in the train that's also
good for performing.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.920
But it's like a different feeling.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.270
- Like the reason I think
I'm able to do this,

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.313
is because I come and I
bring a sincere energy.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.050
- [Nahre] And how long
have you been doing this?

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.110
- Done this actually as
my, basically my day job,

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.680
for like the last five years.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.420
I make better money doing this

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.995
than I certainly did waiting tables.

00:02:44.995 --> 00:02:48.107
And definitely than I did doing a

00:02:48.107 --> 00:02:49.669
lot of other jobs that I only did

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.350
for no reason other than money.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.612
So I mean hell, I was
doing this for free anyway.

00:02:54.612 --> 00:02:56.529
♪ Ohhh ♪

00:02:59.842 --> 00:03:02.092
(clapping)

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:06.843
- I moved to New York City,
to pursue my dream of music.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.620
But I was nannying part time,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.010
I love kids and I was like
this is a good part time job

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.818
that'll allow me to, to still like,

00:03:15.818 --> 00:03:17.640
go to open mics and like, do music.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.520
And I would see people busking and I

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.580
always just thought it
was like so courageous.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.506
And I would see like amazing buskers where

00:03:24.506 --> 00:03:25.339
I was just like wow.

00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:29.210
Like they're so talented and
they were so, like, care free.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.399
And I was just like I
wish I had those guts.

00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:35.272
♪ Light it up ♪

00:03:35.272 --> 00:03:37.552
♪ YOLO is what I believe in ♪

00:03:37.552 --> 00:03:39.880
♪ Fight it up ♪

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.360
Finally I was like, let me try it,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.460
worked up the nerve and I did it.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.990
And I was making more than I was nannying.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.625
So since then I just, I started busking.

00:03:48.625 --> 00:03:51.625
♪ As we light it up ♪

00:04:03.797 --> 00:04:06.630
(upbeat electronic music)

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:07.463
- [Nahre] Here in New York City

00:04:07.463 --> 00:04:09.662
there's a complicated
legal history with busking.

00:04:09.662 --> 00:04:12.880
(upbeat violin music)

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.770
And over the last
century the city has gone

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.013
back and forth on whether or
not it's allowed or banned.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.020
During the great depression,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.800
busking became a popular
way to make money.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.370
That is until Mayor Fiorello
LaGuardia outlawed it in 1936,

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.632
calling the street performers beggars.

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:35.799
(upbeat violin music)

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.090
The ban continued all the way to 1970,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.560
when poet Allen Ginsberg,

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.160
challenged the ban as
unconstitutional and won.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.080
Busking is protected as a form of free

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.180
speech in the U.S.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.750
Meaning it can't be prohibited
in areas where other

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.620
forms of free speech are not prohibited.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.610
But even though busking is
now legal in the subways,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.593
that hasn't stopped the
police from arresting buskers.

00:04:59.593 --> 00:05:01.760
- [Man] You can't do that.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:03.785
- [Yut] Cops are so annoying.

00:05:03.785 --> 00:05:04.618
- Are they?

00:05:04.618 --> 00:05:05.451
- Yeah, even I, even I have the permit,

00:05:05.451 --> 00:05:07.406
they come up to me sometimes.

00:05:07.406 --> 00:05:09.400
It's like it's to loud,
or it's just annoying.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.590
- It was really a struggle,

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.810
if you weren't a sponsored musician,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.260
if you weren't a Music
Under New York Musician,

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.120
you go through that process.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.233
Then yeah it was tough.

00:05:20.298 --> 00:05:23.465
(upbeat violin music)

00:05:25.356 --> 00:05:28.631
♪ I'm trying so hard ♪

00:05:28.631 --> 00:05:31.658
♪ To get by ♪

00:05:31.658 --> 00:05:34.990
♪ I'm trying so hard ♪

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.100
- [Nahre] In 1985 the MTA created

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.270
a program called Music Under New York,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.820
that gives artists a special

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.080
permit to perform in the subways.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.640
For some, this permit is great,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.490
because it allows you to get priority

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.990
at popular locations, and
protection from law enforcement.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.849
- [Nahre] Can you tell us
a little bit about MUNY?

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:52.682
- Yes.

00:05:52.682 --> 00:05:53.570
So Music Under New York is a program

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:55.620
that was created for people
like myself and other

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.753
talented performers through out the city,

00:05:56.753 --> 00:05:59.820
to be able to legally
perform in the subway.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.310
And not, you know, get in
trouble with the police.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:02.610
And so there are
regulations, like you know,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.471
spaces, and places you can
do it and things like that.

00:06:05.471 --> 00:06:07.195
The audition it takes, once a year,

00:06:07.195 --> 00:06:09.240
about they audition about
maybe 300 people this year.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.430
And out of 300 they chose me
as one of the people for this,

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.220
to get into the front round.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.510
- [Nahre] Admittance into the program

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.150
is through one audition held every year.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:21.295
And from the over 300 musical
acts that applied in 2018,

00:06:21.295 --> 00:06:24.270
only 28 were admitted.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.320
This had led many musicians
to criticize the MTA for

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.940
virtually controlling
who can and who can't

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.440
perform in the subway systems.

00:06:32.578 --> 00:06:35.662
♪ Say it yeah ♪

00:06:35.662 --> 00:06:38.330
♪ I'm trying so hard ♪

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:43.330
- MUNY is, is uh, maybe
necessary in some ways

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.090
I suppose if you really
want to not break the law

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.666
technically playing with amplification.

00:06:49.666 --> 00:06:54.474
I kind of enjoy breaking
the law in small ways.

00:06:54.474 --> 00:06:57.116
(laughs)

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:59.384
Maybe you could cut that out.

00:06:59.384 --> 00:07:01.306
- [Nahre] No, I mean I
think that's awesome.

00:07:01.306 --> 00:07:02.628
♪ It starts with today ♪

00:07:02.628 --> 00:07:04.467
♪ No more holdin' me ♪

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:06.325
♪ I'm livin' my life ♪

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.638
♪ No one checks over me ♪

00:07:07.638 --> 00:07:08.998
♪ It starts with today ♪

00:07:08.998 --> 00:07:10.600
♪ No more holdin' me ♪

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.480
- [Nahre] The good news
is that in recent years,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.820
the police have begun to
loosen up on arresting buskers.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.060
This has hopefully
created a more fair space,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.020
where both MUNY artists and independent

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.463
artists can share their
talents with the public.

00:07:25.741 --> 00:07:27.024
♪ I've been a liar ♪

00:07:27.024 --> 00:07:28.581
♪ I've been a thief ♪

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.990
♪ I've done stuff you wouldn't believe ♪

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.890
- Any noticeable differences between when

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.020
you first started performing
on the streets versus now?

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.044
- Yes, absolutely.

00:07:38.044 --> 00:07:41.260
We've had mayoral changes.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.910
So ever since that leadership changed,

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:47.040
I've noticed the law enforcement

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.040
really loosen up on
busting buskers' chops.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:53.053
So to speak

00:07:54.423 --> 00:07:57.506
(upbeat piano music)

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.680
- What motivates you to come out here

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.080
even on cold days like this?

00:08:04.059 --> 00:08:06.233
- It's soul food for me.

00:08:07.469 --> 00:08:12.469
It's necessary, for my
health and well-being, yeah.

00:08:18.187 --> 00:08:21.492
♪ We all grow up ♪

00:08:21.492 --> 00:08:23.867
♪ Oh ♪

00:08:23.867 --> 00:08:26.550
- [Nahre] I like the comparison
of busking to soul food,

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:29.020
because it's the passion
for music that keeps these

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:30.900
artists coming back to perform.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.320
Busking isn't easy and even
though there are many rewards,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.153
there are just as many challenges.

00:08:37.638 --> 00:08:41.471
(singing in foreign language)

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:50.300
- [Nahre] What are some
challenging things about busking?

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:52.730
- Every now and then

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:55.784
I get a kind of a grinch like person.

00:08:55.784 --> 00:08:56.617
(laughs)

00:08:56.617 --> 00:08:57.619
- How do you deal with that?

00:08:57.619 --> 00:08:58.452
- I don't.

00:08:58.452 --> 00:08:59.285
- You don't.

00:08:59.285 --> 00:09:00.118
- I just (laughs).

00:09:00.118 --> 00:09:00.951
- I like that.

00:09:00.951 --> 00:09:02.650
- People don't seem to care anymore,

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:04.510
everybody has headphones on,

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.490
or they're looking at their iPhone.

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:07.937
- You know you're just talking at walls.

00:09:07.937 --> 00:09:10.410
And a lot of people do
talk at walls in this city.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.081
A lot of people totally talk at walls.

00:09:12.081 --> 00:09:14.746
A lot of people are walls to be talked at.

00:09:14.746 --> 00:09:18.579
(singing in foreign language)

00:09:22.304 --> 00:09:24.450
- The most challenging
thing about busking,

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.013
is getting things here.

00:09:26.013 --> 00:09:27.400
- [Nahre] Lugging--

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.990
- Lugging my stuff, yeah lugging my stuff.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.783
- Sound, no electricity.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:36.130
- Playing with gloves on
underneath a grate you know,

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.231
and the cold air is just
like blasting down on you.

00:09:39.231 --> 00:09:40.660
Getting those sympathy tips.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:43.290
- At one point I was singing and people

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:44.970
were gathering and you know,

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.747
applauding me, and really getting into it,

00:09:46.747 --> 00:09:50.030
and singing along and putting
money in my little basket.

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.976
And this one person was
like why are you singing?

00:09:52.976 --> 00:09:53.968
- [Nahre] Oh no!

00:09:53.968 --> 00:09:55.940
- Why do you have it blah blah blah blah,

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.760
you know you sound
terrible blah blah blah.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.650
And I was like, kay.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:02.880
I just, I didn't really
react but she kept going.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.000
And the more she kept saying crazy stuff,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.960
the more people would just
put money in my basket.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.853
So I was hoping she would keep going.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:16.720
(slow acoustic guitar music)

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:31.230
- [Nahre] I also think that busking

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.800
provides a unique space to experiment.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.640
What I mean by that is when
you perform at a concert,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.270
the audience is there to see you,

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.010
and most likely came to hear music.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.330
However when you're busking,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.800
you're performing for
people in their space,

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.260
and they can choose to ignore
you or stop and listen.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.820
- You are imposing on people by being,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.290
it's a public space, and I mean,

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:55.670
you fart on the train
you're imposing on people.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.090
You know, sorry I farted on your world.

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:03.287
But (laughing) it's my
idea of a nice smell.

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:04.628
You know?

00:11:04.628 --> 00:11:05.968
(laughing)

00:11:05.968 --> 00:11:06.801
- Wait what?

00:11:06.801 --> 00:11:07.634
Okay.

00:11:08.494 --> 00:11:11.001
(laughing)

00:11:11.001 --> 00:11:13.091
♪ To let my light shine yeah ♪

00:11:13.091 --> 00:11:15.091
♪ Yeah ♪

00:11:17.306 --> 00:11:18.800
- Do you feel like it's easier to connect

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.480
with people while you're busking versus,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.560
performing on a different
stage or a different venue?

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.160
- I think because the subway allows you

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:30.410
to interact with anyone and everyone,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.660
and anyone and everyone can listen.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.460
It gives you a wider platform to

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:37.130
engage with a wider audience,

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.550
as opposed to someone
that can't afford to pay,

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.200
or who's able to pay to go to a show,

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.080
or whatever it is you know.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:46.197
♪ They say pray pray pray pray ♪

00:11:46.197 --> 00:11:48.653
♪ Oh how you worship ♪

00:11:48.653 --> 00:11:53.100
♪ I wish I could love what you see ♪

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.270
- I started busking because,

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:59.760
I've known since I was little
that I wanted to be a singer.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.010
But as I got older and I was auditioning

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:02.843
and performing more,

00:12:02.843 --> 00:12:06.172
I was suffering from really
bad performance anxiety.

00:12:06.172 --> 00:12:07.180
- Okay.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.280
- So much so that it actually
was getting in my way,

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:13.400
and I was losing like the joy
that I had found in singing.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.920
So that's when I decided
to do something bold

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:17.720
and like kind of like,

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.960
push my comfort zone as best as I could.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.300
And the thing I thought of
was singing in the subway.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.493
- I deal with a lot of stage fright.

00:12:24.493 --> 00:12:25.410
- [Emma] Yeah.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.383
- And I have all my life.

00:12:27.383 --> 00:12:28.216
- [Emma] Yeah.

00:12:28.216 --> 00:12:32.150
- But anytime I remember that it's about

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.210
something that's not within but--

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.043
- [Emma] Right.

00:12:35.043 --> 00:12:36.320
- Outside with the audience.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.153
- Right.

00:12:37.153 --> 00:12:38.703
- It usually gets better.

00:12:38.703 --> 00:12:42.796
♪ I will learn ♪

00:12:42.796 --> 00:12:46.129
♪ To let my light shine ♪

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:50.900
- I have a question
personally about busking,

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:55.750
and playing outside
publicly on the keyboard.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.500
I'm going to try later
on today, with my friend.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.110
Do you have any tips for someone who

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.920
is completely new to busking?

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.050
- Absolutely.

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.330
- And someone who's rather shy,

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:10.223
in terms of doing stuff in public.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.770
- I do, because I was there.

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:15.603
- Okay.

00:13:15.603 --> 00:13:16.550
- And actually I've come a long way,

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:18.850
from when I started busking.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:20.910
It took me at least
five years to figure out

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.160
that you have to interact
with the audience.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.710
Hey if you guys have any requests,

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:26.280
please write them on a fifty dollar bill.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.790
You know, stuff that gets people laughing.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:29.870
I mean you don't have to.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.455
You can all be in your instrument.

00:13:32.455 --> 00:13:33.650
But...

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.490
It's a, it's a huge difference.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.240
I see it in the tips.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:39.800
- [Nahre] And I have a question about

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:40.840
your Venmo sign.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:41.673
- [Emma] Yes.

00:13:41.673 --> 00:13:44.250
- A lot of people don't
carry cash anymore.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:45.083
- Right.

00:13:45.083 --> 00:13:47.400
One day in the middle of a set, you know,

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.120
a girl came up to me and she was like,

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:49.953
oh do you have Venmo?

00:13:49.953 --> 00:13:51.680
And I was like no.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:54.560
And she was like, oh well I
really like what you're doing,

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.060
and I wanna give you a tip
but I don't have any cash,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:57.957
so you should like get a Venmo.

00:13:57.957 --> 00:14:00.760
And I was like that is a fabulous idea.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:02.450
I should get a Venmo.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:06.920
- [Nahre] I met up with my friend

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.160
Dotan at his workspace in SOHO.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.750
He's performed all over the
world with his upright piano,

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.943
in unexpected places like a
subway car and the Eiffel Tower.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:19.070
- Hi my name is Dotan
and today we're gonna be,

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:22.820
we're playing music in
the subway and it's fun.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:23.690
- But you've done this before right?

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:24.751
- Oh yeah.

00:14:24.751 --> 00:14:25.584
- This is my first time.

00:14:25.584 --> 00:14:26.850
- So I used to, I used to walk around and,

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.370
I'm doing such a bad job at this.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:29.332
I'm sorry!

00:14:29.332 --> 00:14:30.165
(laughing)

00:14:30.165 --> 00:14:31.920
- We just set up and we're going

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.053
to busk for about 20 to 30
minutes and see what happens.

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:38.253
The goal is to make five bucks.

00:14:38.253 --> 00:14:39.211
(laughing)

00:14:39.211 --> 00:14:40.044
- Five.

00:14:40.044 --> 00:14:40.877
- Five.

00:14:40.877 --> 00:14:41.710
- That's a, that's a good goal.

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.182
Oh there you go, that's the one.

00:14:43.182 --> 00:14:46.515
(slow electronic music)

00:14:50.252 --> 00:14:51.502
- I'll do this.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.850
And then you solo over it.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:58.183
(slow electronic music)

00:15:19.387 --> 00:15:20.641
- Nice.

00:15:20.641 --> 00:15:23.558
(slow piano music)

00:15:39.623 --> 00:15:41.810
(laughing)

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.863
- Give us money, we need money.

00:15:44.726 --> 00:15:46.380
- I think we need to up the energy.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.300
- I mean part of it's eye contact maybe.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.180
I mean that looks good to me,

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.000
in terms of engagement.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:53.950
We gotta be louder.

00:15:55.506 --> 00:15:59.006
(upbeat electronic music)

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:02.298
- Yeah, keep the energy out here.

00:16:02.298 --> 00:16:05.798
(upbeat electronic music)

00:16:13.077 --> 00:16:14.943
- And a Merry Christmas.

00:16:15.777 --> 00:16:16.944
How you doing?

00:16:19.626 --> 00:16:20.459
Thank you.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:25.453
- [Nahre] The buskers that we

00:16:25.453 --> 00:16:27.930
met this week made it look easy.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:30.173
But in the end we made just two dollars.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:32.593
Here in New York,

00:16:32.593 --> 00:16:34.810
street performers went
from being called beggars,

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:37.210
to an important part of
the subway experience,

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.473
and vital to the city's music culture.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:42.620
- [Yut] busking used
to be looked down upon,

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.703
they're like oh those homeless.

00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:45.610
But now in this time I think people

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:47.440
see busking as oh wow
he's doing an art form.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:49.640
And because buskers in
New York are amazing.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.040
- I think it's important
to understand that

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:56.860
what you are doing as a busker is,

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:01.150
you are giving positive energy
to everyone that hears you.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.740
And sometimes you may uplift someone from

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:06.363
a very dark or negative place.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.290
- Nobody might tip you.

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:12.690
It can get discouraging for sure.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:13.940
But that's an indication that you're

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.690
not doing it for the right reasons.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:23.610
- If one day I was to
look back at these years

00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:27.263
as the best years of my life,

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.473
I'm okay with that, I'd be okay with that.

00:17:32.323 --> 00:17:35.740
(slow electronic music)

